Likutay Halachos - Hilchos Rosh HaShanah 1

@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Cormorant+Garamond:ital,wght@0,400;0,600;0,700;1,400;1,600&display=swap');

:root{--primary:#2c1810;--accent:#8b4513;--gold:#b8860b;--bg:#faf6f0;--bg-alt:#f0ebe3;--border:#d4c5a9;--text:#2c1810;--text-light:#5a4a3a;--highlight:#fff8dc}

*{margin:0;padding:0;box-sizing:border-box}

body{font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.85;color:var(--text);background-color:var(--bg);text-align:justify}

.container{max-width:780px;margin:0 auto;padding:40px 30px 60px}

.main-header{text-align:center;margin-bottom:35px;padding-bottom:25px;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border)}

.main-header .book-title{font-size:15px;letter-spacing:3px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--accent);margin-bottom:8px}

.main-header h1{font-size:32px;font-weight:700;color:var(--primary);margin-bottom:6px}

.main-header .subtitle{font-size:20px;font-style:italic;color:var(--text-light);margin-bottom:4px}

.main-header .halacha-num{font-size:17px;color:var(--accent);font-weight:600}

.main-header .source-ref-header{font-size:15px;color:var(--text-light);font-style:italic;margin-top:6px}

.section-marker{text-align:center;margin:35px 0 20px;font-size:16px;font-weight:700;color:var(--accent);letter-spacing:2px}

.section-marker::before,.section-marker::after{content:"\2014";margin:0 12px;color:var(--border)}

.sub-header{text-align:center;margin:30px 0 18px;font-size:19px;font-weight:600;color:var(--primary);font-style:italic}

p{margin-bottom:16px;text-indent:1.5em}

p.no-indent,.section-marker+p,.sub-header+p,.main-header+p{text-indent:0}

.verse{font-style:italic;color:var(--accent)}

.verse-source{font-size:14px;color:var(--text-light);font-style:normal}

.heb-term{font-style:italic}

.concept{font-weight:600}

.source-ref{font-size:15px;color:var(--text-light)}

.preamble{border-left:3px solid var(--border);padding:12px 20px;margin:0 0 30px;background:var(--bg-alt);font-size:17px;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0}

@media(max-width:600px){.container{padding:20px 16px 40px}body{font-size:16px}.main-header h1{font-size:26px}}

Likutay Halachos &middot; Orach Chaim

Hilchos Rosh HaShanah

Halacha 1

Inyan Shofar &middot; 5 Oaseyos + Inyan HaMai&rsquo;ah Kolos

Based on what Rabbainu, of blessed memory, wrote

in the ma&rsquo;amar &ldquo;T&rsquo;homos Y&rsquo;chasyumu&rdquo; (siman 9)

on the passuk: &ldquo;A window [tzohar] shall you make for the taivah,&rdquo; etc.,

&ldquo;and the opening of the taivah you shall place on its side,&rdquo; etc.,

&ldquo;lower, second, and third [stories],&rdquo; etc.

And the general rule is: when the darkness and the k&rsquo;lipos [husks/shells] surround from every side, the main tikkun [rectification] is through emes [truth] &mdash; that you should see that the dibbur [speech] of the t&rsquo;fillah [prayer] goes out from your mouth in truth. And through this, Hashem, blessed be He, will illuminate for him in the aspect of &ldquo;Hashem ori v&rsquo;yish&rsquo;i&rdquo; [&ldquo;Hashem is my light and my salvation&rdquo;] (T&rsquo;hillim 27:1). And he will merit to make an opening in the darkness in which he is trapped. See there well. And more than what is written there, I heard orally [peh el peh] from his holy mouth. And through this, the tikkun of t&rsquo;fillah is accomplished, which is the aspect of Yaakov and his sons. See there well.

And this is the aspect of the kol [sound/voice] of the Shofar, which hints at t&rsquo;shuvah [repentance], in the aspect of &ldquo;improve [shap&rsquo;ru] your deeds.&rdquo; As is written: &ldquo;Awake, sleepers, from your slumber!&rdquo; And therefore the Shofar, which is the aspect of Alma D&rsquo;Asai [the World to Come], is the aspect of Binah [Understanding], the aspect of t&rsquo;shuvah, as is known. And this is the aspect of the kol that goes out from the Shofar, which is the aspect of a kol of emes [voice of truth] that goes out from the depths of the heart [umka d&rsquo;libba], in the aspect of &ldquo;from the depths I called to You, Hashem&rdquo; (T&rsquo;hillim 130:1), in the aspect of &ldquo;from the narrow place I called to Y-ah&rdquo; (T&rsquo;hillim 118:5). Meaning, due to the great intensification of the one who surrounds from every side, then they turn themselves to the truth and call to Hashem in truth from the depths of the heart &mdash; through which one is saved from the darkness, in the aspect of &ldquo;and the opening of the taivah you shall place on its side,&rdquo; as above.

And this is the aspect of &ldquo;He answered me in the expanse, Y-ah&rdquo; (T&rsquo;hillim 118:5) &mdash; that one merits to be broadened and to go out, as above. For the kol of the Shofar is the aspect of emes, which is the aspect of Yaakov, as is written: &ldquo;Give emes to Yaakov&rdquo; (Michah 7:20), in the aspect of &ldquo;the kol is the kol of Yaakov&rdquo; (B&rsquo;raishis 27:22). And this is what Rabbainu wrote there: that Yaakov is the aspect of t&rsquo;fillah, for the main tikkun of t&rsquo;fillah is through emes, as above, which is the aspect of Yaakov, as above.

And therefore in the Torah only the T&rsquo;ruah is mentioned, and the other kolos [sounds] we learn from there, as Rabbasainu, of blessed memory, said. For the T&rsquo;ruah is the aspect of Yaakov &mdash; the kol of the Shofar fundamentally hints at him, as above. And therefore the T&rsquo;ruah is g&rsquo;nuchi ganach [groaning groans] and y&rsquo;lulai yalil [wailing wails] &mdash; meaning, that which he groans and wails and calls to Hashem to return to Him, as above. And therefore every T&rsquo;ruah has a P&rsquo;shutah [straight blast] before it and a P&rsquo;shutah after it &mdash; this also hints at t&rsquo;shuvah: at first, he was pashut [straight/simple] and rectified &mdash; meaning, before the corruption. And after he was corrupted and broken, in the aspect of &ldquo;disgrace has broken my heart&rdquo; (T&rsquo;hillim 69:21), in the aspect of &ldquo;and they pursued them until the &lsquo;breakings&rsquo;&rdquo; (Y&rsquo;hoshua 7:5) &mdash; then through the T&rsquo;ruah, which is the aspect of emes as above, through this he returns and becomes straightened [nifshatt]. And this is the aspect of the T&rsquo;kiah after it, which is a straight [pashut] sound.

And therefore we blow [the Shofar] on Rosh HaShanah, for then is the intensification of the dinim [judgments], which are the aspect of the darkness. And therefore one needs to draw himself to the emes, which is the aspect of the kol of the Shofar, as above. And it is a remembrance for the first day [zikaron l&rsquo;yom rishon] &mdash; for then, at the beginning of the B&rsquo;riyah [Creation], the darkness intensified, covering the face of the deep [t&rsquo;hom]. And the Ru&rsquo;ach Elokim [Spirit of G-d], which is the aspect of Yaakov &mdash; who is the aspect of ru&rsquo;ach [spirit], as is written: &ldquo;and the spirit of Yaakov their father was revived&rdquo; (B&rsquo;raishis 45:27) &mdash; who is the aspect of emes, as above &mdash; was hovering over the face of the waters, to drive out and clear the darkness through the aspect of emes. And through this, the light was revealed, as is written: &ldquo;and Elokim said, &lsquo;Let there be light,&rsquo; and there was light&rdquo; (B&rsquo;raishis 1:3). As is explained above, that through emes, the light of Hashem, blessed be He, shines &mdash; in the aspect of &ldquo;Hashem ori&rdquo; [&ldquo;Hashem is my light&rdquo;] &mdash; as is explained there, well. See there. And through this, the B&rsquo;riyah came into being.
